<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fall of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1453</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as conquest of Constantinople · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-ottoman-wars">Byzantine–Ottoman wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1453, after a siege in which heavy artillery played a major role. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and made the city the Ottoman capital. The Ottoman recovery that made it possible followed the empire's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1402, a link noted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Fall of Constantinople" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DimiTalen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Constantinople_fresco,_Moldovi%C8%9Ba_monastery,_Vatra_Moldovi%C8%9Bei,_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
